WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:04.080
To start with, I was like, I don't think we can do this.

2
00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:08.000
We intentionally ran really fast and straight into many walls.

3
00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:11.000
But we said, cool, this is going to be warts and all,

4
00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:14.000
alpha software, you can use it.

5
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:18.840
It is hard to convince people that this is safe to use.

6
00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:20.880
And that's scary.

7
00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:23.880
There's, like, a big existential kind of,

8
00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:27.240
you know, interrobang at the end of that sentence.

9
00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:30.760
Your ability to write code is actually not useful.

10
00:00:30.760 --> 00:00:34.760
The agents can do it probably better than you can. And, fingers crossed,

11
00:00:34.760 --> 00:00:37.760
we all have jobs in two years.

12
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:47.280
I don't know how.

13
00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:49.920
I work as part of the agent

14
00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:52.960
enablement team, which specifically focuses

15
00:00:52.960 --> 00:00:58.200
on the Dark Factory, which I believe is what I'm here to talk about today.

16
00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:03.520
A dark factory is

17
00:01:03.520 --> 00:01:07.560
the idea of building software in a more autonomous way.

18
00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:08.840
In a very autonomous way,

19
00:01:08.840 --> 00:01:12.080
in fact, where we are not supervising the software anymore.

20
00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:15.520
And our dark factory goes by the name Kikimora.

21
00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:18.040
The concept of a dark factory is from manufacturing,

22
00:01:18.040 --> 00:01:21.080
and you have a factory building, but the lights are not on

23
00:01:21.080 --> 00:01:22.960
because there are no humans inside.

24
00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:25.000
I don't think that's where software engineering is going,

25
00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:26.920
but it was a kind of forcing function to

26
00:01:26.920 --> 00:01:29.280
say, well, this is the world we think we're going to.

27
00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:33.440
So let's be a bit controversial, I guess, to try and force people to see that

28
00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:36.600
things are rapidly changing and also to encourage debate.

29
00:01:36.760 --> 00:01:40.120
So the very simple version is

30
00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:44.560
you create a Linear issue, the factory picks it up,

31
00:01:44.760 --> 00:01:46.680
it solves it with an autonomous agent.

32
00:01:46.680 --> 00:01:48.360
So it's not an interactive session.

33
00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:51.720
It just does it in the cloud and it opens a GitHub PR.

34
00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:54.640
It then babysits this GitHub PR.

35
00:01:54.640 --> 00:01:59.000
So if you go and comment on it and request some changes, it's going to do that

36
00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:02.000
until you merge the PR.

37
00:02:02.080 --> 00:02:03.400
That's it.

38
00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:06.960
So I think a lot of people have talked about the November period

39
00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:10.120
when Claude Code and other coding agents really start to produce good output.

40
00:02:10.440 --> 00:02:13.840
That is when everybody at the company shifted from,

41
00:02:13.880 --> 00:02:16.120
oh yeah, I'm using coding agents, to,

42
00:02:16.120 --> 00:02:18.400
oh, you're not using coding agents? Why not?

43
00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:21.600
The journey everybody goes on after that, it's just one of bottlenecks, right?

44
00:02:21.640 --> 00:02:23.760
Like, as soon as you start getting faster, you realize, like,

45
00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:25.280
all these other processes

46
00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:29.280
start to break down because they assumed the thing before them was slow.

47
00:02:29.480 --> 00:02:34.280
So I'd say around January, February was where we started hitting big bottlenecks.

48
00:02:34.880 --> 00:02:39.240
Software engineering was speeding up, at least from an external perception

49
00:02:39.240 --> 00:02:40.240
of other companies.

50
00:02:40.240 --> 00:02:41.120
And you got Anthropic

51
00:02:41.120 --> 00:02:44.760
coming out and saying, hey, we rewrote Bun from Zig to Rust in two weeks.

52
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:49.400
And so there was a conversation at the senior leadership level, but

53
00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:54.440
like, cool, what do we need to do to drive our own kind of pace of change up?

54
00:02:54.840 --> 00:02:57.520
Other people are already using software factories inside their companies

55
00:02:57.520 --> 00:02:58.680
to build their products.

56
00:02:58.680 --> 00:03:01.600
We knew that we needed to not be left behind.

57
00:03:01.600 --> 00:03:06.160
We've just had engineers using AI in whatever way works for them.

58
00:03:06.160 --> 00:03:11.600
And I think in practice that was most developers running single sessions

59
00:03:11.600 --> 00:03:17.320
or maybe parallel sessions with Claude Code, still raising PRs kind of in their name

60
00:03:17.320 --> 00:03:20.320
and asking for human review. It was very, like,

61
00:03:20.320 --> 00:03:23.320
AI-enhanced but still very siloed work.

62
00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:28.520
We realized we're not being ambitious enough by quite a long way.

63
00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:32.120
I think there was a very clear moment where we just decided

64
00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:34.120
the only way out is through.

65
00:03:34.120 --> 00:03:37.040
Like, we have to just stop fighting on every frontier,

66
00:03:37.040 --> 00:03:40.920
one at a time, and we have to just say, what's a fundamentally different model

67
00:03:40.920 --> 00:03:46.520
that at least has, like, the solution baked into the problem,

68
00:03:48.040 --> 00:03:48.880
Okay,

69
00:03:48.880 --> 00:03:52.640
so I think everybody is largely here

70
00:03:52.680 --> 00:03:54.840
and everybody may have heard I'm going to talk a little bit

71
00:03:54.840 --> 00:03:58.560
about personal experiments I've been doing with

72
00:03:59.120 --> 00:04:02.960
harness engineering, software factories. It's been a slight change

73
00:04:02.960 --> 00:04:06.600
in topic, and we're actually going to do an improv workshop.

74
00:04:06.640 --> 00:04:08.480
Just kidding. No, no, no, don't worry. We're going to.

75
00:04:08.480 --> 00:04:11.440
I wanted to see who would react the most terrified.

76
00:04:11.440 --> 00:04:13.680
The beauty of being able to work on side

77
00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:17.680
projects is you can take more opinionated, ambitious stances right away.

78
00:04:17.680 --> 00:04:22.000
And so Night Shift was my own internal orchestrator that I made for side

79
00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:26.200
projects, where the goal was, don't look at the code at all.

80
00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:29.200
Night Shift was a good proof

81
00:04:29.200 --> 00:04:32.200
point to leadership that, hey,

82
00:04:32.280 --> 00:04:36.200
there is something there, and it was cool and it was visible and it was real.

83
00:04:36.760 --> 00:04:39.880
I think it was Macey had the brilliant idea

84
00:04:39.880 --> 00:04:44.240
that we all go and build our own dark factory separately, each of us,

85
00:04:44.280 --> 00:04:49.200
and then we get together and create a big Frankenstein of dark factories

86
00:04:49.480 --> 00:04:51.760
that is the best of the best.

87
00:04:51.760 --> 00:04:54.800
This would be ludicrous in traditional software engineering, right?

88
00:04:54.800 --> 00:05:00.040
But in this particular case, given how quickly you can iterate to build

89
00:05:00.200 --> 00:05:04.240
some sort of prototype of that factory, it was really the right decision.

90
00:05:04.720 --> 00:05:07.480
Some people went and they tried off-the-shelf products.

91
00:05:07.480 --> 00:05:10.680
So things that were sort of already building towards factories

92
00:05:10.680 --> 00:05:14.240
and automations, some people built their own from scratch.

93
00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:18.360
And the funny thing was they all came back and they all looked different.

94
00:05:18.360 --> 00:05:20.880
And at first we sort of viewed this as, like, oh crap.

95
00:05:20.880 --> 00:05:23.560
You know, maybe this is just, like, needs to be a full custom rig.

96
00:05:23.560 --> 00:05:25.160
Nobody agrees.

97
00:05:25.160 --> 00:05:29.080
But as we started to sit down and compare notes with each other,

98
00:05:29.120 --> 00:05:31.880
we realized that there were a lot of familiar

99
00:05:31.880 --> 00:05:37.080
or even identical components being built. Next up is Maria.

100
00:05:37.640 --> 00:05:40.640
Maria stole the spotlight a little bit.

101
00:05:40.840 --> 00:05:45.480
She had kind of a mic drop moment where she was like: I built Kikimora with itself.

102
00:05:47.880 --> 00:05:49.280
She basically had

103
00:05:49.280 --> 00:05:52.800
built this orchestrator on, say, what, like a Thursday,

104
00:05:53.040 --> 00:05:56.760
and then told it to improve itself over the weekend.

105
00:05:56.800 --> 00:05:59.720
I mean, that's a dramatic simplification of what happened.

106
00:05:59.720 --> 00:06:03.240
But this orchestrator did it all by itself.

107
00:06:03.560 --> 00:06:06.240
The proof is kind of in the pudding.

108
00:06:06.240 --> 00:06:11.040
It was very easy for anyone to come and try making an issue through this.

109
00:06:11.360 --> 00:06:13.160
They just had to go to Linear and make an issue.

110
00:06:13.160 --> 00:06:17.320
They didn't have to install anything, run anything on their laptop, nothing at all.

111
00:06:17.320 --> 00:06:20.440
It was already running on my laptop. This made it very easy

112
00:06:20.440 --> 00:06:24.080
for people to validate that this kind of works as an interface.

113
00:06:25.040 --> 00:06:27.400
I chose the name.

114
00:06:27.400 --> 00:06:30.680
Kikimoras are Slavic mythical creatures. I'm Slavic, Bulgarian.

115
00:06:30.680 --> 00:06:33.840
I thought I would choose something coming from my culture.

116
00:06:34.240 --> 00:06:36.800
Kikimora is a creature that lives in your home,

117
00:06:36.800 --> 00:06:41.040
and she comes at night and she helps you tidy up.

118
00:06:41.040 --> 00:06:45.880
She helps you with the chores, but only if you're a good host.

119
00:06:46.120 --> 00:06:50.880
If you are lazy, if you don't tidy up, she's actually going to do the opposite.

120
00:06:50.880 --> 00:06:55.400
She's going to mess everything up for you and prank you. I thought

121
00:06:55.400 --> 00:07:00.640
that was an excellent parallel to what I think the Dark Factory is.

122
00:07:01.520 --> 00:07:05.640
It was interesting for the week and a half or so

123
00:07:05.640 --> 00:07:11.040
when the whole team was using Kikimora for the Dark Factory.

124
00:07:11.720 --> 00:07:14.320
I was running inside the Docker container inside Maria's laptop.

125
00:07:14.320 --> 00:07:16.440
Every time she went home, she went on the tube.

126
00:07:16.440 --> 00:07:19.440
She lost internet connection and the thing stopped working for a bit.

127
00:07:19.960 --> 00:07:23.440
A big welcome to AI Native Dev London 2026.

128
00:07:23.440 --> 00:07:25.880
We also had an event around that time.

129
00:07:25.880 --> 00:07:30.200
At some point I wanted to attend a talk and I left my laptop

130
00:07:30.240 --> 00:07:33.200
unattended, and I asked colleagues, please don't close it.

131
00:07:33.200 --> 00:07:36.480
And of course, somebody closed my laptop in the middle of another

132
00:07:36.480 --> 00:07:39.920
member of the team doing something very important through the dark factory.

133
00:07:40.680 --> 00:07:40.880
Yeah.

134
00:07:41.880 --> 00:07:43.080
And you have some issues.

135
00:07:43.080 --> 00:07:46.400
If I could go back again, the one thing I'd change would be

136
00:07:46.520 --> 00:07:50.400
to get more buy-in to this concept of loop engineering being the way forward.

137
00:07:51.160 --> 00:07:52.000
The biggest thing

138
00:07:52.000 --> 00:07:55.720
I think once the software factory got to production and we started using

139
00:07:55.720 --> 00:07:59.120
it, it felt overwhelming in terms of what there is to learn.

140
00:07:59.280 --> 00:08:02.000
It felt like there was kind of a huge barrier to kind of adopting it.

141
00:08:02.000 --> 00:08:05.000
And I definitely know other people felt that as well within the company.

142
00:08:05.080 --> 00:08:09.000
There is such an immense shift you have to make as a software engineer.

143
00:08:09.000 --> 00:08:12.440
If you're going to adopt this factory approach,

144
00:08:12.440 --> 00:08:15.440
at some point you have to flip the switch.

145
00:08:16.120 --> 00:08:17.800
It was a very tight timeline.

146
00:08:17.800 --> 00:08:21.520
So we had three weeks to onboard the whole engineering team.

147
00:08:21.680 --> 00:08:24.480
In fact, Rob Willoughby, I remember that

148
00:08:24.480 --> 00:08:28.400
he went to leadership in the first week to warn them

149
00:08:28.400 --> 00:08:32.480
that we might be behind schedule. Yeah, it felt like a reach.

150
00:08:33.040 --> 00:08:34.720
I mean, we smashed it,

151
00:08:35.680 --> 00:08:38.880
but I wasn't sure that we were going to get there.

152
00:08:39.240 --> 00:08:42.240
It was not trivial to get there.

153
00:08:42.600 --> 00:08:45.320
It was tough and we had to do a lot of work, as I said,

154
00:08:45.320 --> 00:08:48.200
like authentication running in the cloud, all of these things.

155
00:08:48.200 --> 00:08:53.160
But ultimately we had a running prototype before those three weeks were up.

156
00:08:53.480 --> 00:08:56.800
In fact, I would say that's maybe even too long.

157
00:08:57.000 --> 00:09:00.440
It should be like a week to get on board,

158
00:09:00.800 --> 00:09:03.800
three weeks to get used to it.

159
00:09:04.000 --> 00:09:06.280
We really pushed leadership actually

160
00:09:06.280 --> 00:09:10.160
to use it, to show culturally that, hey, this is a bet that we're making.

161
00:09:10.160 --> 00:09:11.640
We want people to use that.

162
00:09:11.640 --> 00:09:15.960
Our CEO, Guy, actually shipped a database migration to prod via

163
00:09:15.960 --> 00:09:19.120
the dark software factory without anyone on engineering knowing about that.

164
00:09:19.520 --> 00:09:24.000
So that was a bit of a, oh, maybe we need some thinking on this.

165
00:09:25.120 --> 00:09:25.640
I think

166
00:09:25.640 --> 00:09:30.040
cultural change on the engineering side is less about velocity and more about,

167
00:09:30.080 --> 00:09:33.800
can I trust the results of this enough to sign my name to this PR, which is what

168
00:09:33.800 --> 00:09:37.800
we're functionally asking people to do. There are a lot of people who are like,

169
00:09:37.800 --> 00:09:40.920
I need to be certain that this will work perfectly.

170
00:09:40.920 --> 00:09:44.880
I'm not going to try it unless I see data that it works perfectly.

171
00:09:44.880 --> 00:09:50.080
And your shift needs to be, okay, I'm going to adopt this way of working,

172
00:09:50.800 --> 00:09:54.520
and then I'm going to make it work perfectly. You can't drip feed that.

173
00:09:54.520 --> 00:09:57.000
You just have to bite the bullet.

174
00:09:57.000 --> 00:09:59.840
We're trying to pitch to engineers who have come up in a discipline

175
00:09:59.840 --> 00:10:03.160
that has said your ability to write code is the most important thing,

176
00:10:03.160 --> 00:10:05.160
because that is something that is unique about you,

177
00:10:05.160 --> 00:10:07.000
and you're very well paid for that.

178
00:10:07.000 --> 00:10:08.840
Now there's a fundamental shift.

179
00:10:08.840 --> 00:10:12.760
Your ability to understand the system, or the way it's all put together,

180
00:10:12.760 --> 00:10:16.280
or the interlocking of technical constraints, business constraints

181
00:10:16.280 --> 00:10:18.720
and all that kind of stuff, that's where your value lives.

182
00:10:21.960 --> 00:10:24.600
I think for me, we've demonstrated that we can move so quickly.

183
00:10:24.600 --> 00:10:26.600
And I wouldn't have believed anyone who said,

184
00:10:26.600 --> 00:10:28.320
you're going to be here in eight weeks time.

185
00:10:28.320 --> 00:10:29.600
I would have been like, what?

186
00:10:29.600 --> 00:10:31.280
We don't even have a software factory,

187
00:10:31.280 --> 00:10:35.040
let alone people using it and let alone all of these capabilities.

188
00:10:35.560 --> 00:10:40.160
The amount of time I spend in an IDE now has exponentially decreased.

189
00:10:40.200 --> 00:10:44.720
Way more than 90% of my work is now being delegated to the, to the cloud.

190
00:10:44.720 --> 00:10:46.880
And then the 10% is largely things

191
00:10:46.880 --> 00:10:49.880
like the front end changes that I want to spend more time crafting.

192
00:10:50.320 --> 00:10:51.720
We are shipping faster.

193
00:10:51.720 --> 00:10:55.040
I think every week or two we go about 30% faster than we did the week before.

194
00:10:55.040 --> 00:10:57.360
And it doesn't seem to be tapering off yet.

195
00:10:57.360 --> 00:11:02.320
The biggest surprise I had was how much it can improve itself

196
00:11:02.320 --> 00:11:04.120
without me doing anything.

197
00:11:04.120 --> 00:11:05.840
If I give it the right data,

198
00:11:05.840 --> 00:11:09.680
the only thing I did was give it simple instruction,

199
00:11:09.720 --> 00:11:14.520
go look at the logs and find general patterns for improvement.

200
00:11:15.040 --> 00:11:15.760
We started off

201
00:11:15.760 --> 00:11:19.480
having one person each day on support, but then pretty quickly we're like, hang on.

202
00:11:19.520 --> 00:11:22.280
We have a software factory that can improve itself.

203
00:11:22.280 --> 00:11:25.320
Let's have it automatically triage the issues.

204
00:11:25.960 --> 00:11:27.320
If you'd said to me

205
00:11:27.320 --> 00:11:29.000
certainly two months ago, but even a month ago,

206
00:11:29.000 --> 00:11:31.720
we would quite easily have been able to try some of these ideas,

207
00:11:31.720 --> 00:11:33.880
I would have been like, I don't think we can do that easily.

208
00:11:37.800 --> 00:11:41.080
We think every company is going to need to own their own factory.

209
00:11:41.080 --> 00:11:45.000
And in fact, we think that is what software engineering is going to become.

210
00:11:45.400 --> 00:11:49.200
Actually, there's a lot of plumbing that goes into building a factory

211
00:11:49.200 --> 00:11:52.680
that, however you shape it, you're going to need.

212
00:11:52.680 --> 00:11:55.520
And it's actually pretty annoying to build.

213
00:11:55.520 --> 00:11:56.400
But what we want

214
00:11:56.400 --> 00:11:59.240
to do is we want to make it really easy to jump to the interesting part,

215
00:11:59.240 --> 00:12:03.760
like the part that's actually going to drive impact for your business. There's

216
00:12:03.760 --> 00:12:09.400
actually a huge opportunity there to say, let's build a factory-building agent.

217
00:12:09.400 --> 00:12:11.560
And so that's where the Tessl agent was born.

218
00:12:11.560 --> 00:12:14.040
In many ways it will look and feel like a coding agent.

219
00:12:14.040 --> 00:12:15.160
You work in your terminal.

220
00:12:15.160 --> 00:12:17.560
It has, like, a headless automated mode.

221
00:12:17.560 --> 00:12:19.520
But its purpose isn't really writing code.

222
00:12:19.520 --> 00:12:25.200
It's helping you identify workflows, extract them from all of your current

223
00:12:25.200 --> 00:12:29.080
silos of working, and then helping you sort of format them for agents,

224
00:12:29.440 --> 00:12:33.320
upload them to our skills registry, which we use to track workflows,

225
00:12:33.400 --> 00:12:38.280
then create automations and improve them. As we brought this concept to customers,

226
00:12:38.280 --> 00:12:42.680
we started to see customers get excited by building their factory.

227
00:12:42.680 --> 00:12:46.920
But we're there to help make it easy, not just the building part,

228
00:12:46.920 --> 00:12:52.000
but also the kind of keeping up. You're never done building the factory.

229
00:12:52.000 --> 00:12:54.800
And so you sort of need a factory copilot.

230
00:12:57.000 --> 00:12:57.640
As the

231
00:12:57.640 --> 00:13:02.440
pace of software development at Tessl accelerated, it always felt like

232
00:13:02.440 --> 00:13:06.400
our website and kind of marketing messages just to sort of not keeping up

233
00:13:06.400 --> 00:13:10.240
with what we're building because we're building a lot.

234
00:13:10.280 --> 00:13:10.560
Right.

235
00:13:10.560 --> 00:13:14.880
And so we moved our messaging framework into the monorepo

236
00:13:14.880 --> 00:13:18.280
so we can auto-generate with the factory

237
00:13:18.280 --> 00:13:21.680
sales collateral or website changes.

238
00:13:21.680 --> 00:13:23.200
So more and more of these factory

239
00:13:23.200 --> 00:13:27.120
capabilities are exposed to the broader organization as a whole.

240
00:13:27.120 --> 00:13:30.680
And it's been amazing to see, not just the productivity boost,

241
00:13:30.680 --> 00:13:34.320
but the excitement that the whole team has around using the factory.

242
00:13:35.000 --> 00:13:38.160
A designer on my team came to me and was like, I just shipped my first

243
00:13:38.200 --> 00:13:41.200
PR and I've never felt more empowered in my life.

244
00:13:41.320 --> 00:13:45.000
And now we have the GTM team using the Dark Factory.

245
00:13:45.040 --> 00:13:48.560
We have the People's Team using the Dark Factory for tasks

246
00:13:48.560 --> 00:13:51.560
that are nothing to do with coding.

247
00:13:51.640 --> 00:13:55.240
As we see our kind of non-development teams

248
00:13:55.400 --> 00:13:59.400
embrace the factory, we are also coming across all sorts of gaps

249
00:13:59.400 --> 00:14:00.600
and things that are harder.

250
00:14:00.600 --> 00:14:04.440
And we've been using those learnings to build those back into the product

251
00:14:04.440 --> 00:14:08.240
itself. And, like, through the course of history,

252
00:14:08.280 --> 00:14:12.200
anytime you find a technology that makes something valuable,

253
00:14:12.200 --> 00:14:16.840
more approachable to a wider set of people, and, like, transition

254
00:14:16.840 --> 00:14:21.080
somebody from doing the thing to enabling others to do the thing,

255
00:14:21.080 --> 00:14:24.720
that is always the way that we drive foundational shifts. And

256
00:14:25.840 --> 00:14:27.160
the reality is, once you do

257
00:14:27.160 --> 00:14:29.960
start using a software factory and it is running a lot of code for you

258
00:14:29.960 --> 00:14:34.120
and you're seeing a lot of success, it does change your mindset and shift

259
00:14:34.120 --> 00:14:37.120
how you think about how software is going to be built.

260
00:14:37.400 --> 00:14:41.120
When I was at AI Engineer earlier this year, Theo gave the closing keynote.

261
00:14:41.120 --> 00:14:45.480
And one of the points that he stressed is that our ambition isn't big enough.

262
00:14:45.800 --> 00:14:49.480
There are so many things there that we can solve so much quicker,

263
00:14:49.480 --> 00:14:52.000
so much faster, that needed entire engineering

264
00:14:52.000 --> 00:14:55.080
teams to be commissioned in order to deliver those products that can

265
00:14:55.080 --> 00:14:58.680
now be delivered by one really strong product minded individual.

266
00:14:59.120 --> 00:15:02.560
Like, there are some really amazing human problems that we're going to be able

267
00:15:02.560 --> 00:15:05.840
to deliver on a lot quicker than we would have been able to.

268
00:15:07.160 --> 00:15:09.160
To me this is the new software engineering.

269
00:15:09.160 --> 00:15:10.600
This is what it looks like.

270
00:15:10.600 --> 00:15:12.440
We haven't figured out the details yet.

271
00:15:12.440 --> 00:15:16.360
And being one of the people who works on it

272
00:15:16.360 --> 00:15:19.360
and figures out those details is extremely exciting.

273
00:15:20.720 --> 00:15:22.760
Faster, better, cheaper.

274
00:15:22.760 --> 00:15:25.560
Pick two. No, in this case, it's all three.

275
00:15:25.560 --> 00:15:27.360
You have them at your fingertips.

276
00:15:27.360 --> 00:15:31.960
It's inevitable that this will be doing all of the implementation work.

277
00:15:31.960 --> 00:15:35.560
Help the process and try to adapt

278
00:15:36.040 --> 00:15:39.040
your thinking into something

279
00:15:39.600 --> 00:15:40.240
bigger.
